president gabriel bordich was keenly  aware of how much was riding on his  first state of the nation address  but it received an ovation when he  announced a national plan for finding  the bodies of thousands of people who  disappeared during chile's military  dictatorship  [Applause]  if we don't know where they are we  cannot heal our wounds  boric will also introduce a fiscal  reform plan to congress next month and a  long-awaited pension reform bill in  keeping with his social agenda he vowed  to build a quarter of a million public  houses  but the main focus was his road map for  addressing chile's most pressing  immediate problems from soaring  inflation aggravated by the war in  ukraine  to the escalating armed conflict with  indigenous mapuche groups it's been the  shortest of hunting moons for latin  america's youngest president whose new  left progressive program had awoken huge  expectations but in only 82 days a  combination of inexperience very bad  luck on the economic front and lack of  strong leadership to tackle violence and  rising crime have seen his approval  ratings plummet  borrich promised to increase the police  and intelligence budgets continue to  deploy the army and the rest of  haraocania region and allocate funds to  compensate those affected by sabotage  acts and organized crime  emulating his new zealand counterpart he  went further  i will introduce a law to congress to  totally prohibit the ownership of  firearms  we have seen what happens in other  countries and we won't allow that to  happen in chile  but the conservative opposition in  congress is unlikely to give boric a  break they thought that a new phase over  generation would make things easy but  it's never easy to govern and in boric's  case it's obvious  but it's recognized that mistakes have  been made but he insisted that he remain  optimistic reminding chileans that they  have a historic opportunity to build a  more equal society  lucia newman al jazeera valparaiso
 

